<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-functions-with-the-same-derivative" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Functions with the same derivative
  </title>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a differentiable real-valued function defined on an interval <m> X </m>. Prove that if <m> f' \of{ x } = 0 </m> for all <m> x \in X </m>, then <m> f </m> is constant.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        By contraposition. If <m> f </m> is not a constant, then <m> f \of{ a } \neq f \of{ b } </m> for some distinct points <m> a , b \in X </m>. We may suppose without loss of generality that <m> a \lt b </m>. Since <m> X </m> is an interval, <m> \clint{ a }{ b } \subseteq X </m>. Thus <m> f </m> is continuous on <m> \clint{ a }{ b } </m> and differentiable on <m> \opint{ a }{ b } </m>. The <xref ref="theorem-mean-value-theorem" text="title"/> now implies that
        
        <md>
          f' \of{ c } = \frac{ f \of{ b } - f \of{ a } }{ b - a } \neq 0
        </md>
        
        for some interior point <m> c \in \opint{ a }{ b } \subseteq X </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f , g \colon X \to \R </m> be differentiable real-valued functions defined on an interval <m> X </m>. Prove that if <m> f' \of{ x } = g' \of{ x } </m> for all <m> x \in X </m>, then <m> f </m> and <m> g </m> differ by a constant.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        This follows immediately by applying part (a) above to the difference <m> f - g </m>. Indeed, we see that
        
        <md>
          \of{ f - g }' \of{ x } = f' \of{ x } - g' \of{ x } = 0
        </md>
        
        for all points <m> x \in X </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The above result will be important in <xref ref="chapter-riemann-integration"/>, where we will use it to compare two anti-derivatives of the same continuous function on a closed interval.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</exercise>
